perature effect nor the variation of the internal resistance\nL182@P3: influential factors like the variations in internal resistance (R) and temperature influence are not\nL183@P3: considered. The capacity fading effect is not taken into account in this model as well.\nL184@P3: 3.2. Lam and Bauer Battery Model (Battery Model 2)\nL185@P3: The Lam and Bauer battery equivalent circuit model is shown in Figure 3. The model\nL186@P3: demonstrates the VOC as a function of SOC, a variable ohmic resistance Ro, and two variable\nL187@P3: RC‐networks: RSCS and RlCl for the short and the long time transient responses, respectively. Lam\nL188@P3: and Bauer also showed the relation between capacity fading due to aging and different stress\nL189@P3: influences, which are the cell’s temperature, C‐rate, SOC and intensity of discharge. Lam and Bauer\nL190@P3: parametrized their equations through curve fitting of experimental measurements. They employed\nL191@P3: in their tests the LiFePO4 battery cell. The equivalent circuit resistors and capacitors equations for\nL192@P3: temperatures from 20 °C and above are detailed in Equations (7)–(28) in Reference [20]. We refer also\nL193@P3: to the VOC equation with the corrected constants as proposed in Reference [20]:\nL194@P3: ܸ୓େሺSOCሻ ൌ െ0.5863\t݁ିଶଵ.ଽ ୗ୓େ ൅ 3.414 ൅ 0.1102 SOC െ 0.1718 ݁ି଼ൈଵ଴షయ\nL195@P3: ଵିୗ୓େ (3)\nL196@P3: Figure 2. Tremblay and Dessaint battery discharge model.\nL197@P3: In case of discharging the output voltage reads:\nL198@P3: Vbatt “ E0 ´ R ¨ i ´ K\nL199@P3: Q\nL200@P3: Q ´ it ¨ pit ` i\nL201@P3: ˚\nL202@P3: q ` Ae´B ¨ it (1)\nL203@P3: and for charging, the equation becomes:\nL204@P3: Vbatt “ E0 ´ R ¨ i ´ K\nL205@P3: Q\nL206@P3: it ´ 0.1Q\nL207@P3: ¨ i\nL208@P3: ˚ ´ K\nL209@P3: Q\nL210@P3: Q ´ it ¨ it ` Ae´B ¨ it (2)\nL211@P3: the variables and constants in Equations (1) and (2) are defined in Table A1 in the Appendix A.1.\nL212@P3: Although, the charging and discharging characteristics are extensively modeled, some other\nL213@P3: influential factors like the variations in internal resistance (R) and temperature influence are not\nL214@P3: considered. The capacity fading effect is not taken into account in this model as well.\nL215@P3: 3.2. Lam and Bauer Battery Model (Battery Model 2)\nL216@P3: The Lam and Bauer battery equivalent circuit model is shown in Figure 3. The model demonstrates\nL217@P3: the VOC as a function of SOC, a variable ohmic resistance Ro, and two variable RC-networks: RSCS\nL218@P3: and RlClfor the short and the long time transient responses, respectively. Lam and Bauer also showed\nL219@P3: the relation between capacity fading due to aging and different stress influences, which are the cell’s\nL220@P3: temperature, C-rate, SOC and intensity of discharge. Lam and Bauer parametrized their equations\nL221@P3: through curve fitting of experimental measurements. They employed in their tests the LiFePO4 battery\nL222@P3: cell. The equivalent circuit resistors and capacitors equations for temperatures from 20 ˝C and above\nL223@P3: are detailed in Equations (7)–(28) in Reference [20]. We refer also to the VOC equation with the corrected\nL224@P3: constants as proposed in Reference [20]:\nL225@P3: VOC pSOCq “ ´0.5863 e\nL226@P3: ´21.9 SOC ` 3.414 ` 0.1102 SOC ´ 0.1718 e\nL227@P3: ´ 8ˆ10´3\nL228@P3-4: 1´SOC (3)Energies 2016, 9, 563 5 of 17 Energies 2016, 9, 563 5 of 18\nL229@P4: Figure 3. Lam and Bauer battery circuit model.\nL230@P4: 3.3. Wijewardana Battery Model\nL231@P4: A different perception, than the two previous models, is adopted in this model. The electrical\nL232@P4: components, RS, CS, RL, CL are functions of SOC and independent of the temperature. Only the series\nL233@P4: internal resistance resistor is a function of SOC and temperature (RintS(SOC,T)). The temperature\nL234@P4: influence is considered by adding potential correction terms, which are voltage due to electrode film\nL235@P4: formation (ΔE) and voltage due to electrolyte electrons transfer formation (ΔVChe). The capacity\nL236@P4: fading effect is modeled as an additional series resistance Rcyc. The battery output voltage is computed\nL237@P4: by subtracting the voltage drop of each circuit element from the VOC value. Figure 4 demonstrates\nL238@P4: Wijewardana battery model.\nL239@P4: Figure 4. Wijewardana battery circuit model.\nL240@P4: The battery output voltage of this model reads [1]:\nL241@P4: ܸୠୟ୲୲ ൌܸ୓େ െ ݅൫ܴ୧୬୲ୗ ൅ܴୡ୷ୡ൯െܸଵ െܸଶ െ ∆ܧሺܶሻ െ ∆ܸେ୦ୣሺܶሻ (4)\nL242@P4: ∆ܧሺܶሻ ൌ ሺ1൅ܥாଵ∆ܶሻ\nL243@P4: ܸ݀௥\nL244@P4: ݀ܶฬ\nL245@P4: ்ୀ்ౙ౛ౢౢ\nL246@P4: ∆ܶ (5)\nL247@P4: ∆ܸେ୦ୣሺܶሻ ൌ βݓ\t݁݌ݔሺିଵ ௧⁄ሻ∆ܶ ൅\nL248@P4: ܸ݀େ୦ୣ\nL249@P4: ݀ܶ ฬ\nL250@P4: ்ୀ்ౙ౛ౢౢ\nL251@P4: ሺܥେ୦ୣ ൅ܥେ୦ୣଵ∆ܶሻሺ1൅βሻ∆ܶ (6)\nL252@P4: The values of the model parameters are listed in the Appendix A.\nL253@P4: 3.4. Assessment of Battery Models Qualities\nL254@P4: Three different highly dynamic to model Li‐ion batteries have been proposed. Each model has\nL255@P4: its dominant features. Table 2 summaries the qualities of each model. The (+) sign implies that the\nL256@P4: corresponded feature is considered in the model. Whereas, a (++) sign denotes an extensive\nL257@P4: consideration to the related feature. In contrast, the (‐) sign means that the related feature was\nL258@P4: assigned as a constant or it is not considered at all in the model. Battery model 2 considers more\nL259@P4: factors than the other models. However, each model must be evaluated with experimental data to\nL260@P4: investigate its accuracy.\nL261@P4: Figure 3. Lam and Bauer battery circuit model.\nL262@P4: 3.3. Wijewardana Battery Model\nL263@P4: A different perception, than the two previous models, is adopted in this model. The electrical\nL264@P4: components, RS, CS, RL, CL are functions of SOC and independent of the temperature. Only the series\nL265@P4: internal resistance resistor is a function of SOC and temperature (RintS(SOC,T)). The temperature\nL266@P4: influence is considered by adding potential correction terms, which 